body:
@limdor your case is a bit different as `__eq__` is an instance method. And there is something a bit more sinister at play: [Name Mangling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Name_mangling#Python)

In essence, the double-underscore-as-private is nothing more than syntactic sugar. The variable _is still accessible_ but it gets renamed. In the case of `__eq__` you don't know the class of `other` so blindly accessing a private attribute may cause an error. Even type hints won't help as they are not enforced at runtime (and I'm not sure that pylint honors type-hints at all).

So in that case, the pylint warning actually _does_ point to a potential bug in the code!

So when not being careful you might expect the following to return `True` (but it actually raises an exeption):

```
class MyClass:

    def __init__(self):
        self.__foo = 10

    def __eq__(self, other):
        return self.__foo == other.__foo


class MyOtherClass:

    def __init__(self):
        self.__foo = 10

    def __eq__(self, other):
        return self.__foo == other.__foo

if __name__ == '__main__':
    a = MyClass()
    b = MyOtherClass()
    print(a == b)
```

... however it raises an `AttributeError` with the following message: `'MyOtherClass' object has no attribute '_MyClass__foo'`

But what about with the attributes that have a single underscore? If we have this example with _foo instead of __foo, it would work but we would get the pylint warning.
Wouldn't be this the way to implement the `__eq__` operator of a custom class?

```
class MyClass:

    def __init__(self, value):
        self._foo = value

    def __eq__(self, other):
        if isinstance(other, self.__class__):
            return self._foo == other._foo
        else:
            return False


class MyOtherClass:

    def __init__(self, value):
        self._foo = value

    def __eq__(self, other):
        if isinstance(other, self.__class__):
            return self._foo == other._foo
        else:
            return False

if __name__ == '__main__':
    a = MyClass(10)
    b = MyOtherClass(10)
    c = MyClass(10)
    print(a == b)
    print(a == c)
```

What about private variables of other instances of the same class? E.g.:
```python
class Foo:
    def __init__(self, child: 'Foo'):
        self.child: 'Foo' = child
        self._private: int = 1

    def do_thing(self):
        self.child._private += 1  # This should not raise a warning
```
